 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9ba6f288-55b6-582e-8aa1-d15ec442bd9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125013 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f39262c6-06ce-51e6-92a2-5f2acfb813a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125013 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3-\\lambda  \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3)+2(2\\lambda -2)-3(2\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda )+(4\\lambda -4)-(6\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1061 20093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f05d37d-484a-5ae0-8ed9-0b46584f4a7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1061 20094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9edf1314-60a8-581e-afc3-f39ecd8b7951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1061 20095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37883812-d28d-5bd5-97af-2df4b9f73f66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125409 #edb/ref 1061 20096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125409 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8240de1-8637-5d84-886a-269d69011445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125409 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1061 25951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c9b6469-fe8d-5b75-bb95-efbf2684e9ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/21/21e0994519fbba32a84020ce4661e98600c1307947083ddc3afe9902658143c1.svg)\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1065 3564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "535c5b8b-f815-5ded-8ae5-668bac420af7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125786 #edb/ref 1064 "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{red}4}$ is a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2.$ \n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that \n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218